<description>
Replace a corner L with a same-color right-triangle outline in the corresponding corner of an enlarged canvas.

Input structure:
- The input is a square grid with one background color and one foreground color.
- The foreground is a one-pixel-thick L inside a square bbox.
- The L touches exactly one horizontal grid edge and one vertical grid edge, identifying a corner.

Rule:
- Fill a square output canvas of side 3n-2, where n is the input side length, with the background color.
- Double the L leg length to get the triangle side length.
- Draw the two perpendicular triangle legs on the same output corner edges and connect their free endpoints with the diagonal.

Key insight:
- The input L encodes the corner, color, and side length of the triangle outline; the missing side is the diagonal between the L's two free endpoints.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="7e4d4f7c" lines="9-24">
<description>
Compress the L-shaped cue to three rows and add a pink copy of the top-row foreground mask.

Input structure
The grid has a dominant background color, a two-color top row, and all
foreground pixels below the top row lie in the first column as vertical markers.

Rule
Keep the input top row as output row 0 and the first marker row as output row 1.
Output row 2 is background except at the top-row foreground columns, which are
recolored pink.

Key insight
The lower repeated marker rows only identify the vertical cue; the generated
row is a recolored mask of the horizontal cue in the top row.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="9ba4a9aa" lines="75-90">
<description>
Return the ring object reached by following the guide path from the checkerboard object.

Input structure
- The input has one dominant background color, one 3x3 checkerboard object,
  several 3x3 ring objects, and sparse guide pixels on a two-cell lattice.

Rule
- Find the checkerboard object and its unique adjacent guide pixel.
- Follow guide pixels of that color through two-cell horizontal/vertical steps.
- Output the unique 3x3 ring object adjacent to that connected guide path.

Key insight
- The checkerboard object is the source, and the color of its attached guide
  pixel selects the path whose far endpoint points at the answer ring.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="6165ea8f" lines="74-90">
<description>
Build a color-labeled shape-equivalence table from the objects listed on the right edge.

Input structure:
- The background contains one main 4-connected object for each foreground color.
- The rightmost column has one singleton pixel of each foreground color, listed top-to-bottom.
- Those singleton pixels define the row and column order of the output table.

Rule:
- Normalize each main object to its tight mask and compare masks up to rotation or reflection.
- Draw 2x2 color labels across the top and down the left, separated by one background row/column.
- For each off-diagonal table cell, paint a 2x2 red block when the two labeled objects have the same shape under rotation/reflection, otherwise paint a 2x2 grey block.
- Leave diagonal cells and all separators as background.

Key insight:
- Object positions and original orientations are distractors; the relation is dihedral shape equivalence, while the right-edge legend fixes the table order.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="eval" puzzle_id="64efde09" lines="68-83">
<description>
Copy colored control-ray ports from one rectangular sprite orientation to every matching transformed sprite.

Input structure:
- bg is the most common color.
- Several dense 2-by-N or N-by-2 rectangular sprites are dihedral transforms of one template.
- Isolated single-pixel control markers sit on rays projected outward from the dominant long edge of some sprites.

Rule:
- For each sprite, the active edge is the long side whose cells have the strongest same-color majority.
- A control marker seen first along an outward ray from an active-edge cell assigns its color to that template cell.
- On every transformed sprite, draw that assigned color outward from the corresponding active-edge cell through bg cells until blocked or out of bounds.

Key insight:
- The marker positions are not absolute; they label cells of a shared rectangular template, then the labels follow each sprite's rotation/reflection.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="eval" puzzle_id="a395ee82" lines="9-24">
<description>
Stamp translated copies of a monochrome template guided by a singleton pixel layout.

Input structure:
- bg = background. A large monochrome shape (template, color T).
- Singleton pixels elsewhere: exactly one is color T (layout origin); the rest share color O (overlap color).
- Singletons sit on a uniform-spacing lattice.

Rule:
- Convert each singleton's offset from the origin into coarse lattice units.
- Place a template copy at each offset, scaled by template height/width.
- The origin's copy is painted O instead of T.
- Any pixel covered by two or more copies is also recolored to O.

Key insight: The single T-colored singleton identifies both the origin position and the "self" copy, which is uniquely recolored.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

